{"name":"Global Warming","hex":"#f1d7d3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"global warming","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sustained increase in the average temperature of the Earth, sufficient to cause climate change."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/global_warming"}],"entries":[{"title":"Global Warming (Pitbull album)","blocks":[{"text":"Global Warming is the seventh studio album recorded by Cuban-American rapper Pitbull. It was released on November 16, 2012 through Mr. 305, Polo Grounds and RCA Records. A teaser for the release was first released onto Pitbull's official Facebook and YouTube channel on September 17, 2012. The production on the album was handled by multiple producers including TJR, Afrojack, Sir Nolan, DJ Buddha, Adam Messinger and Nasri. The album also features guest appearances by Christina Aguilera, Usher, Kesha, Chris Brown, Enrique Iglesias, Jennifer Lopez and Shakira among others."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6d/Global_Warming_album.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Warming_(Pitbull_album)","wikidata":"Q80322"},{"title":"Global Warming (Sonny Rollins album)","blocks":[{"text":"Global Warming is a studio album by jazz saxophonist Sonny Rollins, released in 1998 on Milestone Records. \nThis album peaked at No. 25 on the US Billboard Traditional Jazz Albums chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1a/Global_Warming_%28album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Warming_(Sonny_Rollins_album)","wikidata":"Q3108977"},{"title":"Climate change","blocks":[{"text":"Present-day climate change includes both global warming—the ongoing increase in global average temperature—and its wider effects on Earth's climate system. In a broader sense, climate change also includes previous long-term changes to Earth's climate. The modern-day rise in global temperatures is driven by human activities, especially fossil fuel burning since the Industrial Revolution. Fossil fuel use, deforestation, and some agricultural and industrial practices release greenhouse gases. These gases absorb some of the heat that the Earth radiates after it warms from sunlight, warming the lower atmosphere. Earth's atmosphere now has roughly 50% more carbon dioxide, the main gas driving global warming, than it did at the end of the pre-industrial era, reaching levels not seen for millions of years."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Change_in_Average_Temperature_With_Fahrenheit.svg/330px-Change_in_Average_Temperature_With_Fahrenheit.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_change","wikidata":"Q125928"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg/500px-Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg/1280px-Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg/500px-Bobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABobcat_Fire%2C_Los_Angeles%2C_San_Gabriel_Mountains.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Bleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg/500px-Bleached_colony_of_Acropora_coral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig